🧭 How to Use (Step-by-Step)
Prerequisites (both Windows & macOS)

Create an Azure DevOps PAT (Personal Access Token)

Scopes required: Code (Read) and Project and Team (Read)

Copy the token somewhere safe (you’ll paste it into the script when running).

Know your Org URL

Format: https://dev.azure.com/<your-org>

Example: https://dev.azure.com/akzonobeldw

⚠️ Don’t leave it as https://dev.azure.com/$—replace with your actual org.

🪟 Windows – with PowerShell ISE (and PowerShell 7 optional)

PowerShell ISE runs Windows PowerShell 5.1. It’s fine for this script.
If you prefer the latest PowerShell 7, you can install it too (recommended), but you’ll run it in pwsh or VS Code (ISE doesn’t host PS7).

1) Install PowerShell ISE (if not already installed)

Windows 10/11 (Optional Features UI):
Settings → Apps → Optional Features → View features → search “PowerShell ISE” → Install.

Via PowerShell (Windows 10/11):

Get-WindowsCapability -Online | Where-Object Name -like 'Microsoft.Windows.PowerShell.ISE*'
Add-WindowsCapability -Online -Name Microsoft.Windows.PowerShell.ISE~~~~0.0.1.0


Windows Server:

Install-WindowsFeature PowerShell-ISE

(Optional) Install PowerShell 7

winget (Windows 10/11):

winget install --id Microsoft.PowerShell -e


After install, you’ll have pwsh (PS7) alongside Windows PowerShell (5.1).
You can run this script from either.

2) Download the script

Save Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1 into a folder, e.g., C:\Tools\azure-devops-commit-insights.

3) Update the CONFIG section (top of the script)

In ISE (or any editor), set:

$OrgUrl   = "https://dev.azure.com/<your-org>"
$Pat      = "YOUR_PAT"                 # or leave blank to be prompted securely
$Projects = "Cloud and Data Competence Center"  # or "" for ALL projects
$FromDate = "2025-10-01"
$ToDate   = "2025-11-01"
$OutDir   = "."


Tip: If you don’t want your PAT in the file, leave $Pat = "". The script will prompt you securely.

4) Unblock the script (if downloaded from the internet)
Unblock-File .\Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1

5) Run it

Windows PowerShell ISE (5.1):

Open ISE → File → Open → Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1 → Press F5 (Run).

Windows PowerShell or PowerShell 7 terminal:

# (Optional, session-only) allow script execution
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass

# Run
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1
# or with PS7:
pwsh -File .\Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1

6) Review outputs

The script prints the generated CSV file paths at the end, like:

ado_commits_<timestamp>.csv

ado_unique_contributors_<timestamp>.csv

ado_unique_committers_<timestamp>.csv

plus project/org breakdowns

Open them in Excel/Power BI/etc.

🍎 macOS – using PowerShell 7 (pwsh)
1) Install PowerShell 7 with Homebrew
brew update
brew install --cask powershell


Then start it:

pwsh

2) Download the script

Put Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1 in a working folder, e.g., ~/dev/azure-devops-commit-insights.

3) Update the CONFIG section

Open in any editor (VS Code recommended):

$OrgUrl   = "https://dev.azure.com/<your-org>"
$Pat      = "YOUR_PAT"   # or leave blank to be prompted
$Projects = ""           # leave empty to scan ALL projects
$FromDate = "2025-10-01"
$ToDate   = "2025-11-01"
$OutDir   = "."

4) Run it with pwsh

From the folder containing the script:

pwsh -File ./Get-Ado-Contributors.ps1


macOS/Linux don’t use Windows execution policies, so no Set-ExecutionPolicy needed.

5) Review outputs

CSV files will appear in your $OutDir (default current folder).

✅ Quick sanity checks

Org URL is a real org (e.g., https://dev.azure.com/akzonobeldw), not a placeholder.

PAT has Code (Read) and Project and Team (Read) scopes.

Date range actually has commits.

If $Projects is set, the names match exactly (or leave it empty to scan all accessible projects).

🧯 Troubleshooting
Symptom	Likely Cause	Fix
401 Unauthorized / auth errors	PAT expired/invalid or wrong scopes	Create a new PAT with required scopes and paste again
No projects found or access denied	Wrong $OrgUrl or insufficient permissions	Use correct Org URL and ensure you can view projects in Azure DevOps
Empty CSVs	No commits in date window or project filter mismatch	Widen date range or clear $Projects to scan all
Invoke-WebRequest error (Windows)	Corporate proxy / TLS settings	Try PowerShell 7 (pwsh) which uses newer HTTP stack; or set proxy env vars
🔐 PAT safety tips

Prefer prompting (leave $Pat = "") on shared machines.

If you commit this repo publicly, never commit real PATs.

Consider using an environment variable and reading it in the script if you later automate.
